First of all, thank you!

Second, re: performance improvements:
The performance is currently pretty bad and I'm open to anything to improve it. As a first:

One of the operations currently performed on TCP packets is base64 decoding the bytes, solely for the purpose of getting the size, so as to increment the sequence numbers.

However, rather than allocating memory and copying the base64 bytes, it seems like something that should be able to be determined statically, with something like:

```c
size_t base64_decoded_length(const char *base64_input) {
    if (!base64_input) return 0;

    size_t input_len = strlen(base64_input);
    size_t padding = 0;

    /* Count padding characters at the end */
    if (input_len >= 2) {
        if (base64_input[input_len - 1] == '=') padding++;
        if (base64_input[input_len - 2] == '=') padding++;
    } else if (input_len >= 1 && base64_input[input_len - 1] == '=') {
        padding++;
    }

    /* Each 4 base64 chars represent 3 bytes of data */
    return (input_len / 4) * 3 - padding;
}
```

And then calling it via:
```c
const gsize payload_len = base64_decoded_length(base64_bytes);
```

Do you know if there is any such function already present in Wireshark?
